    "description": "Connects Codex to a Halofy kernel for persistent, tiered agent memory - remember and recall over MCP, with REST as fallback.",
    "definition": "# Halofy Codex Memory Skill\n\nThis skill connects Codex to a Halofy kernel for persistent, tiered agent memory.\n\n## Prerequisites\n- A Halofy deployment and an agent badge key (format `hm_agt_...`)\n- Environment variables set:\n  - `HALOMEM_URL` - the kernel base URL (e.g. `http://localhost:8787`)\n  - `HALOMEM_API_KEY` - your agent badge key\n\n**If these variables are not set**, ask the user to export them. The key is minted by an owner/admin; identity (namespace + actor + role) is resolved server-side from it - never put `namespace` or `actor` in any request body.\n\n## MCP setup - the primary path\n\nCodex speaks Streamable HTTP MCP natively. Merge the server into `~/.codex/config.toml` (or `$CODEX_HOME/config.toml`) - write the URL literally; Codex does not expand env vars in `url`:\n\n```toml\n[mcp_servers.halomem]\nurl = \"http://localhost:8787/mcp\"\nbearer_token_env_var = \"HALOMEM_API_KEY\"\n```\n\nIf the Halofy repo is checked out, the installer does this idempotently:\n\n```bash\nnode kernel/integrations/codex/install.mjs --url $HALOMEM_URL\ncodex mcp list   # verify\n```\n\nThen use the `mem_*` tools directly: `mem_write`, `mem_search`, `mem_fault`, `mem_read`, `mem_stats`. The curl blocks below are the fallback when MCP is unavailable.\n\n## Session - ALWAYS use one\n\nAt the start of the conversation, generate ONE id (your agent name + a unix timestamp) and pass it as `session` in every `mem_write` call. Sessions are provenance - they group what you stored so later recalls can attribute it. `session` is an optional string on write only; it is not a resource you create or query.\n\n## Operations\n\n### Remember - Store knowledge\nWhen the user shares important information worth preserving:\n```bash\n# Default: store with your session provenance - always include your session id\ncurl -X POST $HALOMEM_URL/v1/mem/write \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $HALOMEM_API_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"scope\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"<the knowledge to store, plain text>\", \"session\": \"<session-id>\", \"hints\": {\"type\": \"fact\"}}'\n```\nThe write path runs extraction, verification, dedupe, and supersedence server-side - you just send content. `scope` is one of `user`, `assistant`, `team`, `org`; default to `assistant` (or `user` for facts about the person you're helping). Never overwrite: corrections are new writes that supersede the old fact.\n\n### Store for the whole team/org (ONLY when explicitly asked)\nUse a wider scope only when the user explicitly asks to store something permanently / shared:\n```bash\ncurl -X POST $HALOMEM_URL/v1/mem/write \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $HALOMEM_API_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"scope\": \"org\", \"content\": \"<the knowledge to store>\", \"hints\": {\"verbatim\": true}}'\n```\nThere is no separate \"direct to graph\" lane and no file upload - every write goes through the same governed path; content is plain text.\n\n### Recall - Retrieve knowledge\nBefore answering questions, check if relevant knowledge exists:\n```bash\ncurl -X POST $HALOMEM_URL/v1/mem/search \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $HALOMEM_API_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"query\": \"<user question>\", \"k\": 8, \"tokenBudget\": 2000}'\n```\n\nFor a recall-or-miss lane, use the fault syscall instead - `/v1/mem/fault` with the same body returns `{hit, source, injected, latencyMs}` and may carry a ticket in `liveStatus.ticket` (redeem via `POST /v1/mem/live-result`) when `live` is enabled.\n\n### Check memory stats\n```bash\ncurl -s -X POST $HALOMEM_URL/v1/mem/stats \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $HALOMEM_API_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{}'\n```\nThere are no datasets to list - the key's hierarchical namespace is the grouping (a namespace sees itself and its `/`-prefix ancestors).\n\n## Behavior\n1. At session start, verify `HALOMEM_URL` and `HALOMEM_API_KEY` are set; if not, ask the user to export them. Ping first: `GET $HALOMEM_URL/healthz` is unauthenticated; a 401 on any authed call means a bad key.\n2. At session start, generate one session id for the conversation (your agent name + a unix timestamp) and pass it as `session` in every write.\n3. Use `mem_search` (or `mem_fault`) before answering to check for relevant context.\n4. Use `mem_write` (with `session`) to store important information; only when the user explicitly asks to share or store permanently, widen `scope` to `team` or `org`.\n5. Brown out, never hard-fail: if the kernel is unreachable or returns a JSON `{error, code}`, say memory is degraded and keep working."
