    "skillKey": "halomem-digest-to-slack",
    "name": "halomem-digest-to-slack",
    "description": "Synthesize Halofy memory into a digest and post it to Slack - recall on a topic, format for Slack, confirm the destination, then post.",
    "definition": "# Digest to Slack\n\nTurn memory into a shared update: recall what the kernel knows on a topic, synthesize a concise digest, and post it to Slack. Use it for \"summarize what we know about X and post it to #channel\" or recurring \"what changed\" updates.\n\nThis skill pairs Halofy recall (to gather and synthesize) with Slack delivery.\n\n## Before you start\n- A **Slack connector** must be available to you (an MCP server or tool that can post to Slack). If it isn't, ask the user to connect Slack, then continue.\n- Halofy must be reachable so you can recall. Check connectivity first: `GET $HALOMEM_URL/healthz` is unauthenticated; a 401 on any `POST /v1/mem/*` call with `Authorization: Bearer $HALOMEM_API_KEY` means the key is bad. If the kernel is unreachable, say memory is degraded and ask before continuing - don't invent the digest contents.\n\n## The workflow\n1. **Recall from Halofy.** Recall knowledge on the topic: `POST /v1/mem/search` with `{query, k, tokenBudget}` returns scored refs with content; for a quick recall-or-miss check use `POST /v1/mem/fault` (MCP equivalent: `mem_search` / `mem_fault`). If recall comes up thin, say so in the digest rather than padding - ground it in real memory, never invented detail. Identity comes from the API key server-side; never put `namespace` or `actor` in a request body.\n2. **Format for Slack.** Slack posts use **mrkdwn**, not full Markdown: `*bold*`, `_italic_`, `` `code` ``, `> quote`, and bullet lines with `•` or `-`. Links render as `<https://url|label>`. Keep it scannable: a short headline, key points as bullets, and source links where they exist - a digest, not a wall of text.\n3. **Pick the destination.** Resolve the target by **discovering the channel by name** (or confirm a DM/person). Decide whether it should be a **new message** or a **reply in an existing thread**. If the channel is ambiguous, ask.\n4. **Confirm, then post.** Show the drafted message and the destination, get explicit go-ahead, then post.\n5. **Report back** where it went (a permalink if available) so the user can verify.\n6. **Write the digest back (optional).** `POST /v1/mem/write` with the digest text as `content` and `scope: \"team\"` (or `\"org\"`) so the next \"what changed\" run has it in memory; the kernel handles extraction, dedupe, and supersedence itself.\n\n## Why confirm first\nPosting to Slack is visible to other people. Always confirm the target channel and show the draft before sending; don't pick a channel on your own if it's ambiguous.\n\n## How to behave\n1. Require a Slack connector; if absent, ask the user to connect it first\n2. **Recall and ground** the digest in real memory (`mem_search`/`mem_fault`) - no invented detail; if Halofy is degraded, say so instead of guessing\n3. Keep it concise and **format as Slack mrkdwn** (headline + bullets + `<url|label>` links), not a wall of text\n4. Resolve the destination channel by name (new message vs thread reply); ask if unclear\n5. **Confirm the destination and show the draft before posting**; report the permalink afterward so the user can verify"
